---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const githubUrl = "https://github.com/jinscodes/OTC_crawler";
const baseUrl = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.endsWith("/")
  ? import.meta.env.BASE_URL
  : `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/`;
const findings = [
  ["01", "Primary finding", "Replace this with the most important quantitative result from the paper."],
  ["02", "Robust evaluation", "Explain how DoseBench reveals behavior existing benchmarks do not capture."],
  ["03", "Practical insight", "Summarize the result that matters most to researchers and practitioners."],
];
---

<BaseLayout title="DoseBench — Research Project" description="DoseBench: a benchmark for rigorous evaluation">
  <header class="site-header">
    <a class="brand" href="#home">DoseBench</a>
    <nav aria-label="Primary navigation">
      <a href="#home">Home</a><a href="#abstract">Abstract</a><a href="#benchmark">Benchmark</a>
      <a href="#method">Method</a><a href="#findings">Findings</a>
      <a href="#implications">Implications</a><a href="#cite">Cite</a>
      <a class="github-link" href={githubUrl} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label="View DoseBench on GitHub" title="GitHub">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 .7a11.5 11.5 0 0 0-3.64 22.4c.58.1.79-.25.79-.56v-2.23c-3.22.7-3.9-1.37-3.9-1.37-.53-1.34-1.29-1.7-1.29-1.7-1.05-.72.08-.71.08-.71 1.16.08 1.78 1.2 1.78 1.2 1.03 1.77 2.7 1.26 3.36.96.1-.75.4-1.26.73-1.55-2.57-.29-5.27-1.29-5.27-5.68 0-1.26.45-2.29 1.19-3.09-.12-.29-.52-1.46.11-3.05 0 0 .97-.31 3.16 1.18a10.93 10.93 0 0 1 5.76 0c2.2-1.49 3.16-1.18 3.16-1.18.63 1.59.23 2.76.11 3.05.74.8 1.19 1.83 1.19 3.09 0 4.4-2.71 5.38-5.29 5.67.42.36.79 1.06.79 2.14v3.17c0 .31.21.67.8.56A11.5 11.5 0 0 0 12 .7Z" /></svg>
      </a>
    </nav>
  </header>

  <main>
    <section class="panel hero" id="home"><div class="section-inner hero-grid home-paper">
      <div class="home-details">
        <p class="eyebrow conference-label"><span aria-hidden="true"></span>WWW 2027</p>
        <h1>Can I Take Another Dose? <em>DoseBench:</em> Evaluating LLM Decision-Making in OTC Dosing QA</h1>
        <p class="authors"><span>Maroof Kousar<sup>*</sup></span><span>Jinsung Han<sup>*</sup></span><span>Mohit Chandra</span><span>Munmun De Choudhury</span><span>Yibo Hu</span></p>
        <p class="equal-note"><sup>*</sup> Equal contribution</p>
        <p class="institution"><img class="institution-logo" src={`${baseUrl}images/illinois-tech-logo.png`} alt="Illinois Tech" />Illinois Institute of Technology</p>
        <p class="conference-info">Proceedings of the ACM Web Conference 2027 (WWW '27)<br />May 10–14, 2027 · Dublin, Ireland</p>
        <div class="home-links">
          <a class="home-button" href="#">Paper</a><a class="home-button" href="#">arXiv</a>
          <a class="home-button" href={githubUrl} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 .7a11.5 11.5 0 0 0-3.64 22.4c.58.1.79-.25.79-.56v-2.23c-3.22.7-3.9-1.37-3.9-1.37-.53-1.34-1.29-1.7-1.29-1.7-1.05-.72.08-.71.08-.71 1.16.08 1.78 1.2 1.78 1.2 1.03 1.77 2.7 1.26 3.36.96.1-.75.4-1.26.73-1.55-2.57-.29-5.27-1.29-5.27-5.68 0-1.26.45-2.29 1.19-3.09-.12-.29-.52-1.46.11-3.05 0 0 .97-.31 3.16 1.18a10.93 10.93 0 0 1 5.76 0c2.2-1.49 3.16-1.18 3.16-1.18.63 1.59.23 2.76.11 3.05.74.8 1.19 1.83 1.19 3.09 0 4.4-2.71 5.38-5.29 5.67.42.36.79 1.06.79 2.14v3.17c0 .31.21.67.8.56A11.5 11.5 0 0 0 12 .7Z" /></svg>Code</a>
          <a class="home-button" href="#">Data</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figure class="visual figure-one">
        <img
          src={`${baseUrl}images/figure-1.png`}
          alt="A real Reddit dosing question where the LLM incorrectly answers yes, alongside DoseBench accuracy, false approval, and ambiguous recall ranges."
        />
        <figcaption>A real OTC dosing failure and results across eight LLMs.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div></section>

    <section class="panel abstract-panel" id="abstract"><div class="section-inner abstract-layout">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">Abstract</p><h2>The research in one clear story.</h2></div>
      <div class="abstract-copy">
        <p>Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used to answer everyday health questions online, including whether someone can take another dose of an over-the-counter (OTC) medication. Yet this common safety-relevant setting remains underexplored in medical QA. Answering these questions requires checking previous doses, timing, and product-specific dosing limits, while recognizing when important information is missing.</p>
        <p>We introduce DoseBench, a focused benchmark of 230 consumer-style acetaminophen and ibuprofen dosing questions with human-annotated YES, NO, and AMBIGUOUS labels, and evaluate eight LLMs with five responses per question. Overall accuracy ranges from 44.8% to 62.2%, while models incorrectly approve 14.0% to 60.5% of prohibited doses. Models with similar accuracy can therefore make very different dosing decisions.</p>
        <p>Additional analyses show that these failures are not easily fixed by giving models more guidance. Asking models to check dose, timing, and missing information makes them more cautious, while providing drug facts improves accuracy but can make them overlook missing information. These findings highlight a broader challenge in health QA: applying rules correctly and recognizing when information is missing.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="#benchmark">Continue to the benchmark <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
      </div>
    </div>
      <figure class="abstract-figure">
        <p class="eyebrow">Overview</p>
        <img
          src={`${baseUrl}images/figure-2.png`}
          alt="DoseBench workflow from 230 OTC questions and human annotation through eight model responses and evaluation."
        />
      </figure>
      <div class="abstract-contributions" id="contribution">
        <p class="eyebrow">Contribution</p>
        <h2>Three Core Contributions</h2>
        <div class="contribution-grid">
          <article>
            <div class="contribution-icon" aria-hidden="true">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M7 3h10v4H7V3Zm-2 2H3v16h18V5h-2v4H5V5Zm3 8h8v2H8v-2Zm0 4h5v2H8v-2Z" /></svg>
            </div>
            <h3>A focused benchmark</h3>
            <p>DoseBench provides 230 OTC dosing questions with human gold labels, six question categories, documented dosing facts, and annotation rules.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <div class="contribution-icon" aria-hidden="true">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M12 2 4 5v6c0 5.1 3.4 9.8 8 11 4.6-1.2 8-5.9 8-11V5l-8-3Zm-1 14-4-4 1.4-1.4 2.6 2.6 4.6-4.6L17 10l-6 6Z" /></svg>
            </div>
            <h3>A safety-aware evaluation</h3>
            <p>An evaluation across eight models shows that similar overall accuracy can mask large differences in false approvals and recognition of ambiguous cases.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <div class="contribution-icon" aria-hidden="true">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M4 19h17v2H2V3h2v16Zm3-3-1.4-1.4 4.2-4.2 3 3 5.8-6L20 8.8l-7.2 7.4-3-3L7 16Z" /></svg>
            </div>
            <h3>An analysis of model behavior</h3>
            <p>DoseBench identifies where models struggle and examines how additional guidance and provided drug facts change their dosing decisions.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="panel benchmark-panel" id="benchmark"><div class="section-inner benchmark-shell">
      <div class="benchmark-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Benchmark</p>
        <h2>The DoseBench Dataset</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="benchmark-content">
        <figure class="dataset-table-card">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Benchmark composition</th><th>Count</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Total OTC questions</td><td>230</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Permitted doses <span>YES</span></td><td>74</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Prohibited doses <span>NO</span></td><td>114</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Missing information <span>AMBIGUOUS</span></td><td>42</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Question categories</td><td>6</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <figcaption>DoseBench gold-label distribution and benchmark scope.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div class="benchmark-details">
          <p class="benchmark-intro">DoseBench is a focused benchmark for evaluating whether LLMs can make safe over-the-counter dosing decisions from consumer-style questions about acetaminophen and ibuprofen.</p>
          <article>
            <h3>Consumer-style questions</h3>
            <p>Questions are based on public Reddit discussions, existing public datasets, and original scenarios designed around realistic dosing decisions.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Human gold labels</h3>
            <p>Two annotators assigned YES, NO, or AMBIGUOUS labels using documented Drug Facts, reaching 96.5% raw agreement and Cohen’s κ = 0.945.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Structured safety coverage</h3>
            <p>Six categories cover dose timing, rolling 24-hour totals, multiple medications, repeated dosing, missing information, and dose safety.</p>
          </article>
          <div class="benchmark-note">Eight LLMs produce five responses per question, enabling evaluation of accuracy, safety, ambiguity handling, consistency, and confidence.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div></section>

    <section class="panel panel-dark" id="method"><div class="section-inner"><p class="eyebrow">Method</p><h2>From real evidence to rigorous evaluation.</h2>
      <div class="process"><article><span>1</span><h3>Collect</h3><p>Describe the source data and inclusion process.</p></article><article><span>2</span><h3>Structure</h3><p>Explain how cases and evaluation targets are created.</p></article><article><span>3</span><h3>Evaluate</h3><p>Show how models are tested and responses are scored.</p></article><article><span>4</span><h3>Validate</h3><p>Summarize expert review and quality assurance.</p></article></div>
    </div></section>

    <section class="panel panel-accent" id="findings"><div class="section-inner"><p class="eyebrow">Findings</p><h2>Three results worth remembering.</h2>
      <div class="finding-grid">{findings.map(([number, title, body]) => <article class="finding-card"><span>{number}</span><h3>{title}</h3><p>{body}</p></article>)}</div>
    </div></section>

    <section class="panel panel-light" id="implications"><div class="section-inner split implications-layout">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">Implications</p><h2>What DoseBench changes for the field.</h2></div>
      <div class="implication-list"><article><span>For researchers</span><p>Explain which research questions the benchmark makes measurable.</p></article><article><span>For developers</span><p>Describe how the findings guide safer and more reliable systems.</p></article><article><span>For the community</span><p>State what resources are released and how others can contribute.</p></article></div>
    </div></section>

    <section class="panel cite-panel" id="cite"><div class="section-inner cite-layout">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">Cite</p><h2>Use DoseBench in your research.</h2><p class="body-copy">Replace this placeholder with the final paper citation after the arXiv update.</p></div>
      <pre><code>{`@article{dosebench2026,
  title   = {DoseBench: ...},
  author  = {Author One and Author Two},
  journal = {arXiv preprint},
  year    = {2026}
}`}</code></pre>
      <div class="cite-links"><a class="button primary" href="#">Paper</a><a class="icon-button" href={githubUrl} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 .7a11.5 11.5 0 0 0-3.64 22.4c.58.1.79-.25.79-.56v-2.23c-3.22.7-3.9-1.37-3.9-1.37-.53-1.34-1.29-1.7-1.29-1.7-1.05-.72.08-.71.08-.71 1.16.08 1.78 1.2 1.78 1.2 1.03 1.77 2.7 1.26 3.36.96.1-.75.4-1.26.73-1.55-2.57-.29-5.27-1.29-5.27-5.68 0-1.26.45-2.29 1.19-3.09-.12-.29-.52-1.46.11-3.05 0 0 .97-.31 3.16 1.18a10.93 10.93 0 0 1 5.76 0c2.2-1.49 3.16-1.18 3.16-1.18.63 1.59.23 2.76.11 3.05.74.8 1.19 1.83 1.19 3.09 0 4.4-2.71 5.38-5.29 5.67.42.36.79 1.06.79 2.14v3.17c0 .31.21.67.8.56A11.5 11.5 0 0 0 12 .7Z" /></svg><span>GitHub</span></a></div>
      <footer><span>DoseBench</span><span>Authors · Institution · 2026</span></footer>
    </div></section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
